<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Areopagus</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Council of the Areopagus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Areopagus was the ancient council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, named after the hill north-west of the Acropolis where it met. Composed of former archons, it originally had wide powers of oversight, but the reforms of Ephialtes in 462 BCE reduced it largely to a court for homicide and religious cases. It survived into Roman times, and the apostle Paul is said to have spoken before it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/areopagus.jpg" alt="Areopagus" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: O.Mustafin · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Areopagus_hill.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
